  "verbalization": "Osugacestat is implicated in the pathogenesis of HaCaT cells through a multi-step mechanism involving UDP-glucuronosyltransferase 2A1. This enzyme is a target of multiple compounds, including Acetoxyisovalerylalkannin and (2R,3R,4S,5R)-2-[[(2S)-7-hydroxy-1-(4-hydroxy-3-methoxyphenyl)-3-(hydroxymethyl)-6-methoxy-1,2,3,4-tetrahydronaphthalen-2-yl]methoxy]oxane-3,4,5-triol, all of which ultimately target HaCaT cells. The presence of these targets suggests that Osugacestat may exert its effects through the inhibition of UDP-glucuronosyltransferase 2A1, which in turn may modulate cellular processes in HaCaT cells. The evidence supports a direct interaction between Osugacestat and HaCaT, mediated via UDP-glucuronosyltransferase 2A1, and the observed scores indicate a plausible, though not fully conclusive, relationship. The mechanism is supported by the presence of multiple targets in the pathway, which collectively suggest a potential role in cellular signaling or metabolism in HaCaT cells.",
